  Issue body actions
  
  After setting a large `history-limit`, as scrollback is filled with 78 column lines, tmux becomes increasingly sluggish to complete `refresh-client -S` (and everywhere else), but only if there are subshells in status format (`ie #(command ...)`). Eventually tmux becomes unusable and 100% cpu, by about 40M lines of scrollback, even with no output or interaction and just one tmux window open.
  
  The performance is fair enough (for that many lines) when there is no status-left defined. I do not understand why scrollback size and presence of subshells in status format are linked and cause this incredible slowdown.
  
  1. build latest master on linux with --enable-utempter --enable-static --enable-utf8proc --disable-systemd --sysconfdir=/etc
  2. create rcfile:
  
  ```
  set-option -g history-limit 2147483647
  set-option -g status-left-length 99
  
  set-option -qg \
  status-left "\
  #(git -C #{pane_current_path} \
    symbolic-ref --short HEAD) \
  #(git -C #{pane_current_path} \
    status -uno --porcelain \
    | awk '{if \(!seen[$1]\) {printf\($1\); seen[$1]=1}}')\
  #(git -C #{pane_current_path} \
    rev-list --walk-reflogs --count refs/stash 2>/dev/null) \
  "
  ```
  
  3. start a new tmux
  4. run
  
  ```
  perl -le 'for ($i = 0; $i < 10000000; $i++) {print "a" x 78}'
  ```
  
  5. try `time refresh-client -S`
  6. repeat steps 3 and 4, yielding following run times:
  
  
  10M lines: 0.13s
  
  
  20M lines: 0.22s
  
  
  30M lines: 0.31s
  
  
  40M lines: 0.42s
  
  by that time cpu fans are going, takes 66 seconds to enter copy-paste mode, with lines 0/39999999 shown, scrolling painfully slow, then stops interaction altogether, cpu 100%. you can wait a long time and it seems to never stop. You can interact with tmux, just everything is very slow, on timescale of minutes or tens of seconds even for tmux prompt input to echo
  
  _without_ **status-left** as above,`refresh-client -S` completes instantly, no matter how many lines of scrollback!! Note that `pane_current_path` does not need to be inside git directory for this to happen, or for tmux to have ever been inside one from start.
  
  Not sure if this is related to [#2551](https://github.com/tmux/tmux/issues/2551) or not. After this test, it takes several minutes to exit the test window and full cpu is being used the whole time. But it will exit properly and do orderly shutdown.
  
  If not exiting, Tmux never recovers interactive performance after this, even if all windows that had scrollback are closed, and new empty ones opened instead, it will always be very slow even several hours later and continue to use high cpu even with one window, no scrollback, running a shell doing nothing.
  
