  The primary cause of the slowdown is the virtual size of the tmux server. Your 40 million lines (78 chars + newline) requires, at a minimum, ~2.9 GB of memory. That memory requires [PTEs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Page_table) (page table entries) and related data structures. The exact amount of kernel memory required for the PTEs depends on the system architecture (i386, x86\_64, ARM64, etc.) and the OS. But a rule of thumb is one PTE for every 4 KB of virtual memory. So your 40 million line scrollback buffer probably requires at least 771485 PTEs. Assuming each PTE is 4 bytes that is 3 MB of kernel memory. Every time tmux forks to run an external command (e.g., `git` in your `status-left` definition) the kernel has to copy that 3 MB of memory. This is why the `vfork` syscall was invented. It, however, has its own problems which is why `posix_spawn` was created (and Linux has `clone`).
  
  Until tmux uses `posix_spawn` (on platforms where it is available) you need to limit the scrollback history to something more reasonable. Such as `set -g history-limit 10000` that I've had in my tmux config for many years and found more than adequate.
  
  P.S., On 32-bit architectures your `history-limit` is likely to cause the tmux server to run out of virtual memory and crash when `malloc` fails even if your system otherwise has more than 4 GB of physical memory.
  
