  In case it wasn't obvious my analysis of the memory required for that absurdly large scrollback buffer glosses over a lot of details. For example, on typical UNIX systems, on i386 and x86\_64 architectures, the PTEs are arranged in a hierarchy so that the PTEs that map individual VM pages for a process are themselves grouped into blocks of PTEs that require a PTE. Those additional PTEs obviously require memory but that is typically an order of magnitude (or two) less than the primary PTEs and thus can be ignored for a quick back of the napkin calculation.
  
  [![krader1961](https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/7545917?u=5b3156f2e720d6eddba1f776daf1f1c760d2a7a0&v=4&size=80)](https://github.com/krader1961)
  
