  > primary cause of the slowdown is the virtual size of the tmux server
  
  At 20M scrollback lines, rss is 13G (which seems large for the dataset, but setting that aside), and there is enough memory on my system at this size. The effect seems linear... it's close to half as slow and half as much memory when scrollback is 10M lines. I turned swap off just to confirm paging is not an issue here also (but it was at 40M, see below)
  
  > \[leading to large number of PTEs\]
  
  Yes my system has 4k pages, this means larger page sizes would help then? Hmm, I will try to get my system to use larger pages (2M?) for tmux process, since my processor and kernel support it, and see if this changes things. I may have to use a different malloc() though: I tried transparent\_hugepage `enabled=always`, `defrag=always` and used `GLIBC_TUNABLES=glibc.malloc.hugetlb=2` in the tmux environment when starting server (and my glibc is 2.35 so this should work), but still could not see any huge pages allocated in the tmux `/proc/<pid>/smaps` file...
  
  > \[those PTEs get copied when forking subshell for the status expansions\]
  
  The default `status-interval` is 15s, this is only one window in entire tmux lifetime, not making any new ones (which force status to redraw and thus forks to be made). How many times will these forks be happening? It's not that frequent. Watching strace with fork-following, I can see a few dozens `execve()` happening at the status-interval, which does not seem to account for this. Even copies of large number of PTEs cannot be taking this long for so few occasions.
  
  I did find it was being affected by VM at the tail end of the range, but disabling swap entirely, the slowdown effect is still large at both 10M and 20M lines and makes tmux feel quite slow to add these delays to everything like new window (0.15s at 10M lines, 0.24s at 20M lines).
  
  Even after all windows are gone and all scrollbacks empty, these delays are still present. Tmux never recovers.
  
  > \[large history-size is illegitimate\]
  
  I'm nowhere near the history limit and not describing a problem at those limits, but much lower ones (10M lines. should that be illegal in your world? 10M line limit?) Does it preallocate or something? I think being able to keep a few millions of lines without it becoming so slow is completely reasonable, I have frequently run into situations using terminals that scrolled off which I missed important errors when I was detached for a while, debug info that was way more voluminous than I thought, long running sessions that collect lots of data and otutput, grepping through things, I search later for stuff, etc.
  
  Why are you governing my scrollback size and determining "legitimacy" on my behalf? These are not ridiculous numbers, 10M lines is not ridiculous and effect is easily seen here. I'd rather be explicit and kill the window when it has too many lines of scrollback, or have OOM/crash kill it if I've not managed my windows, that's fine with me. A terminal should be able to handle 10M lines without slowing down to 150ms new window open or pane switch delay.
  
  Please also note again that this _doesn't happen from the scrollback size alone_. Tmux is fast with the same scrollback sizes above, when`status-left` is not set. It happens from when having these `#()` in my `status-left`. And those are only doing a few dozens of forks so even if it's copying many MB of PTEs I don't see why that takes quarter of a second.
  
