  I had thought that fork() created a COW version of the PTEs' own backing pages, I didn't realize the tables were "duplicated" for the new process. I'm still shocked that this duplication takes so long, but it appears to be the case.
  
  Thank you both for the discussion and suggestions. I will find a sweet spot with larger pages and lower scrollback limit for now, and think about a way to inject into variables used by status from out-of-band. I think a control mode connection listening for events could work (since I have to replace triggering from status-refresh itself, I will have to duplicate the triggers that would have caused a refresh, such as window-change).
  
  It looks like this `posix_spawn()` \-\- which can utilize page tables of parent without copy -- would be a better answer (besides setting a lower scrollback size) but I cannot attempt this now. If this changes in future I will reopen.
  
