  [@nicm](https://github.com/nicm): Note that modern shells like [fish](https://fishshell.com/) and the venerable Korn shell (ksh) use `posix_spawn`. They do so in a mostly race free manner by calling `tcsetpgrp` after `posix_spawn`. In practice the race fails rarely and, fish at least, has hacky code to retry and thus further reduce the likelihood of the race being visible. I am not a fan of that code (I contributed to Fish for a few years) but it does seem to work well enough for a popular non-POSIX shell and a widely used POSIX shell. See [fish-shell/fish-shell#3149](https://github.com/fish-shell/fish-shell/issues/3149) where I argued its use should be removed. See also [https://www.mail-archive.com/ast-developers@research.att.com/msg00719.html](https://www.mail-archive.com/ast-developers@research.att.com/msg00719.html).
  
  It's not clear whether the added complexity, and risks, associated with using `posix_spawn` are justified for tmux. Personally, I would argue "maybe". The `posix_spawn` syscall was created for the benefit of programs with a huge virtual size, like the Oracle RDBMS, that occasionally need to run an external program. It wasn't intended for command shells (which arguably includes tmux). Which is why that API lacks proper support for managing the controlling tty.
  
  P.S., Long ago I was a fan of `ksh`. I now recommend not using it because it has a huge number of bugs, including at least one serious security bug that can be trivially exploited by an attacker that is unlikely to ever be fixed.
  
